---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Research Questions" sideBarActiveItemID="research-questions">
  <div class="mb-5 text-center">
    <div class="text-5xl w-full font-bold">Research Questions</div>
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto mt-4 mb-4 h-[2px] w-7/8 bg-white opacity-80"></div>

  <section class="px-4 pb-12 md:px-10 xl:px-16">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-6">
      <article class="rounded-xl border border-base-300/70 bg-base-200/25 p-6 md:p-8 shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-primary md:text-xl">
          When can children coordinate decisions in groups?
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-base-content/90">
          By age 3. Communication predicts their success (Rivero, Vasil, &amp; Tomasello, under review).
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-xl border border-base-300/70 bg-base-200/25 p-6 md:p-8 shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-primary md:text-xl">
          Would young children exploit more if they cared more about the goal?
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-base-content/90">
          Currently investigating this question with Ben Pitt, Carolina Deus, and Dorsa Amir! We think so.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-xl border border-base-300/70 bg-base-200/25 p-6 md:p-8 shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-primary md:text-xl">
          How do adults influence children's response after instructions go away?
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-base-content/90">
          Currently investigating this question with Julia Leonard, Mike Tomasello, and Dorsa Amir! We think
          that children who build a block set with a directive adult will persist less after pictorial directions go away
          compared to those who build with a questioning adult or alone.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-xl border border-base-300/70 bg-base-200/25 p-6 md:p-8 shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-primary md:text-xl">
          How do children learn differently from peers versus adults?
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-base-content/90">
          Currently investigating this question with Mike Tomasello! We think that instruction from peers
          may constrain children's exploration less than instruction from adults.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rounded-xl border border-base-300/70 bg-base-200/25 p-6 md:p-8 shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-primary md:text-xl">
          Why do children 'reset' more than adults? What are the differences between children's and adults' creativity?
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-base-content/90">
          Currently investigating this question with Dorsa Amir! During piloting of a creative building task, we observed a curious pattern.
          Children tended to fully disassemble their previous creation before starting a new one,
          while adults tended to iterate on their previous creation. We are currently investigating what the function of 'resetting' may be,
          in addition to the differences between children's and adults' creativity.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>